<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taksony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 950–970</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taksony was Grand Prince of the Hungarians from about 955 to about 970, the period after the defeat at the Lechfeld that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids on western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>, he is said to have settled Pecheneg and Muslim groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. His son Géza succeeded him and began the conversion of the Hungarians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taksony.jpg" alt="Taksony" width="330" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kálti Márk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taksony_(Chronicon_Pictum_033).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Taksony, also called Taxis or Tocsun, was according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a>, written around 1200, the son of Zoltán and of an unnamed daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a>, a local ruler defeated by the conquering Hungarians shortly before 907. Whether Menumorut was an actual person is debated. The same source says Zoltán abdicated in his favour three years before his own death.</p><p>Modern historians challenge this account of his youth. Liudprand of Cremona, a nearly contemporaneous writer, reports that Taxis came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with a large army in 947, which suggests that he was born considerably earlier than the date the Gesta gives. Around 950 the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus</a> wrote that Fajsz, Taksony's cousin, was then grand prince of the Hungarians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A later writer, Johannes Aventinus, says Taksony fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> on 10 August 955, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> routed an army of 8,000 Hungarians. If so, he was one of the few Hungarian leaders to survive. Historians including Zoltán Kordé and Gyula Kristó suggest that Fajsz abdicated in his favour around that time. After the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-invasions-of-europe">Hungarian raids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> stopped and the Hungarians had to retreat from the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> and Traisen rivers, though they continued their incursions into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> until the 970s.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a> records that a great host of Muslims arrived from the land of Bular under Taksony, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> came too, to whom he granted land from the region of Kemej as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>. The contemporary Ibrahim ibn Yaqub recorded Muslim merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 965. Liudprand writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a> consecrated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Zacheus, in 963 and sent him to the Hungarians to preach that they should attack the Germans, but there is no evidence that Zacheus ever arrived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Taksony died in the early 970s, after arranging the marriage of his elder son Géza to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarolt">Sarolt</a>, daughter of Gyula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. The names of two of his sons, Géza and Michael, are preserved. His own wife is said in the Gesta to have come from the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, an anachronism; historians think it preserves a memory of a Turkic origin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a>, Pecheneg or Volga Bulgarian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taksony of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The grand princes then centralised power: <b>Taksony</b> and especially Géza, ruling from Esztergom, curbed tribal chiefs and invited Western missionaries.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47,19&amp;year=960" aria-label="Hungary on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4996 513 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Taksony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5446 783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47,19&amp;year=960">What was here in 960 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
